// A DFA with more states than fit in a byte. The repetition expands to a chain of one state per repeated character, so
// scanning the input walks through state indices past 127 and past 255. A backend which stores its transition table in
// an 8 bit element type, or which indexes it with a signed byte the way Java would, wraps around along that chain and
// stops matching.
//
// The 32767 boundary of a 16 bit element type is deliberately not covered here. It needs an automaton of more than
// 32768 states, and the construction cost grows sharply with the state count.
//
// Nothing here is interesting about the shape of the automaton, only its size, so the rule is the simplest one which
// reaches that size.
//
// The parser is not what this case is about, so the grammar is the single empty production the file format requires.

@scanner {
    LONG: /a{300}/;
}

@parser {
    tokens
        : @empty
        ;
}
